<section class="section" aria-labelledby="cycle-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="cycle-title">What a working month looks like</h2>
      <p>Ask any provider to show you their version of this. The order matters more than the exact dates, which you should set together, working back from your filing dates.</p>
    </header>
    <ol class="cycle">
      <li><h3>You send</h3><p>Bank statements, bills and notes, using an agreed method.</p></li>
      <li><h3>They record</h3><p>Entries are made and items they cannot classify are listed.</p></li>
      <li><h3>You answer</h3><p>You clear queries promptly. Delay here causes most missed dates.</p></li>
      <li><h3>They reconcile</h3><p>Bank, parties and, if in scope, GST and TDS records are matched.</p></li>
      <li><h3>You review</h3><p>You receive reports and approve anything to be filed.</p></li>
      <li><h3>They file, if in scope</h3><p>Filings are made, and acknowledgements are shared with you.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="split-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="split-title">Who is responsible for what</h2>
      <p>Use this as a starting draft for your written scope. The right-hand column is the one people forget.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Responsibility split, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">You</th><th scope="col">Provider</th><th scope="col">Decide in advance</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Source documents</th><td>Complete and on time</td><td>Chase what is missing</td><td>What happens if they arrive late</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Accuracy of entries</th><td>Answer queries honestly</td><td>Record and check</td><td>Who fixes errors, and at whose cost</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Returns</th><td>Remain responsible for what is filed</td><td>Prepare, and file if engaged</td><td>Who approves before filing</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Payments</th><td>Approve and make them</td><td>Tell you what is due</td><td>Whether they ever get bank access</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Software and data</th><td>Own the licence and data</td><td>Work in it under a named login</td><td>Backups and storage</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Reports</th><td>Read them and act</td><td>Deliver on the agreed date</td><td>Which reports, and in what format</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="hand-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="hand-title">Handing over without a gap</h2>
      <ol class="mini-steps test-steps">
        <li>Choose a cut-off date and get closing balances from your current person.</li>
        <li>List every recurring filing and who has been doing it.</li>
        <li>Move logins and the data file into your name and contact details.</li>
        <li>Give the provider separate access, not your password.</li>
        <li>Run the first month with extra checking, then remove old access.</li>
        <li>Note any open notices or disputes, so nothing is lost.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Compared with hiring</h2>
      <dl class="fit-list">
        <div><dt>Outsourcing tends to fit when</dt><dd>Volume does not justify a full-time role, records are digital, and you want a repeatable process.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Hiring tends to fit when</dt><dd>There is daily on-site work such as bills, cash or stock, and enough of it to fill the role.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>A mix tends to fit when</dt><dd>Someone inside records daily, and an outside professional reviews and files.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="para">A fuller comparison is in {% routeLink "/guides/bookkeeping-vs-outsourced-accounting/", "bookkeeping vs outsourced accounting" %}.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="agree-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="agree-title">Agree these in writing</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Services included and excluded</li>
        <li>Volume limits, and what happens above them</li>
        <li>Monthly dates for each side</li>
        <li>Named contact, and who reviews the work</li>
        <li>Confidentiality and where data is stored</li>
        <li>Notice period, and what is handed back at the end</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>What affects the fee</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Number of transactions, accounts and registrations</li>
        <li>Backlog to clear first</li>
        <li>Stock, branches or payroll in scope</li>
        <li>Frequency and depth of reports</li>
        <li>Filings included, and any notice work excluded</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">AccountOrbit does not set fees. Compare quotes on the same written scope.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="limit-title">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h2 id="limit-title" class="first-h2">What outsourcing does not change</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Your legal responsibility.</strong> Your returns remain yours, even when someone else prepares them.</li>
      <li><strong>Reserved work.</strong> A statutory audit, for example, must be done by an eligible independent auditor. See {% routeLink "/guides/accountant-vs-chartered-accountant/", "who can do what" %}.</li>
      <li><strong>The need to look at your numbers.</strong> Reports that no one reads protect no one.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
